#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e9a46e6-fbd7-5405-9402-e9fc0047ccf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334566} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5f80ec-3a43-5624-bc0b-43e47a7494b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334567} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6f4488-6cc5-538f-a565-b8ac6cffef44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334568} {:answer/id #uuid "723b769e-a6bd-53f1-bd52-58f138ebd19c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334569} {:answer/id #uuid "39ea09d4-69b3-5406-8754-367858191628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e9a46e6-fbd7-5405-9402-e9fc0047ccf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334566} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eecd04d-b3de-5834-bf4c-562fcb4318f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5345f4d0-20b2-5008-8103-b715a5705623" :question/math-academy-id "q-117291" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nThe integrand ${e}^{1-{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){e}^{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}-{e}^{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}-{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{1-{0}^{2}}-{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-0}-0 \\\\  & =-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35d734a7-fa06-598e-bc02-367c8cda50a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334573} {:answer/id #uuid "12c0e594-5c1d-559b-849f-55cab6bd1351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt[3]{1-e}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334574} {:answer/id #uuid "08383a5e-262e-5ecf-a09a-8ac9d8a98df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334575} {:answer/id #uuid "04d22ae3-2fba-515f-8199-72512c411d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[3]{1-e}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334576} {:answer/id #uuid "108d0a5a-a0fc-593b-9c3f-43c46566491e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08383a5e-262e-5ecf-a09a-8ac9d8a98df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334575} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f7fba06-d2fb-5a65-b4a9-dbf68dcbd802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "47a1e353-0abf-5596-bc63-479c1e9efffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-117292" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{e}^{2x}}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-{e}^{2x}}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-{e}^{2x}}^{a}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{-{e}^{2x}}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $-{e}^{2x}$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{-{e}^{2x}}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{-{e}^{2x}}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =-(-2{e}^{2x})\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{3}+1}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\sqrt[3]{(-{e}^{2x})^{3}+1}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\sqrt[3]{1-{e}^{6x}}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=-{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =2{e}^{2(0)}\\sqrt[3]{1-{e}^{6(0)}}+\\sqrt[3]{{0}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt[3]{1-1}+\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6c9710b-2cc6-58b2-887f-9f935486e343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e^x\\sin(e^{2x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6c9710b-2cc6-58b2-887f-9f935486e343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e^x\\sin(e^{2x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334580} :answer-field/id #uuid "83489642-2ffa-5db2-a79f-78b742327bab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4b317c5-e5ec-5e4f-8ea0-05929d43147f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333583" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ for $g(x)={\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{3}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{3}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{3}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(({e}^{x})^{2})) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2x})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab1b4b0a-da51-5ac9-af15-8f312d3c35ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2 - \\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076384} {:answer/id #uuid "ff39b9eb-b57f-53b9-87a0-a22f8e4e1c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2 - x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076385} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9af31e-5cf7-5e3e-9de2-e597988637d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-\\sqrt{x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076386} {:answer/id #uuid "7d79b997-c759-59b0-9b27-d32ba7b51c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2 - \\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076387} {:answer/id #uuid "7f86cc6b-6889-5b12-a8bc-66e6d76f8785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f86cc6b-6889-5b12-a8bc-66e6d76f8785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076388} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cebfe24-5a0e-56a5-86d7-0c580acb43e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3da59ba-9fd7-5e38-87bc-bd24039d3bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-4555" :question/problem "For $x>2$, find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[2,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5075a64-0f91-58cf-b01b-5b71035f0680" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus by Interchanging the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "910d7a2d-690e-54d1-8815-c962dfda7f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(5)=-51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334583} {:answer/id #uuid "fa77798b-86c7-50f7-9460-8b847644bcea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(5)=43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334584} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfbc517-7955-5a72-be04-6f8116b76b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(5)=39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334585} {:answer/id #uuid "6da3aa3e-1a39-5e03-a56c-9ccc60bd4012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(5)=50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334586} {:answer/id #uuid "1611b5c7-2808-5940-8ef1-0071982d690e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(5)=-49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "910d7a2d-690e-54d1-8815-c962dfda7f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(5)=-51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334583} :answer-field/id #uuid "59b24bc6-6bec-5d87-a059-3b98d93075da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04689b09-69a7-501a-b2fc-c0ba3b87804e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75220" :question/problem "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+5x+1.$ Find ${g}^{′}(5)$ given the function is defined by $g(x)={\\int }_{x}^{7}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\int }_{x}^{7}({t}^{2}+5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{7}^{x}({t}^{2}+5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{7}({t}^{2}+5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{7}^{x}({t}^{2}+5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{7}^{x}({t}^{2}+5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+5x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${g}^{′}(5),$ we substitute $x=5$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(5) & =-({5}^{2}+5\\cdot 5+1) \\\\  & =-(25+25+1) \\\\  & =-51\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
